          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does adherence mean in the context of diabetes treatment, and why does it matter?",
          "answer": "In the context of diabetes treatment, adherence refers to how well a person follows their prescribed diabetes management plan, which often includes taking medications and making lifestyle changes. Adherence is crucial because diabetes is largely self-managed, meaning that individuals play a significant role in managing their condition between clinical visits. When adherence is poor, it can lead to suboptimal health outcomes, including elevated blood sugar levels and an increased risk of complications.\n\nThe challenges of adherence are acknowledged to typically stem from factors such as medication costs, complexity of the treatment regimen, side effects, low health literacy, depression, and simply forgetting to take medications. Addressing these issues through structured education and support can improve adherence, resulting in better glucose control and an improved quality of life for the individual (source: \"Sticking with a diabetes regimen: monitoring, medication and review\").\n\nI did not cover specific adherence strategies or individual factors affecting adherence in detail.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
